  v2: copper not corroding: copper sits above hydrogen in the activity series (E° +0.342 V), so in aerated neutral water it is the cathode of any corrosion cell rather than the anode: oxygen takes electrons at its surface and it stays as the metal. The green on an old copper contact is not rust and is not this reaction: it is a patina of basic copper carbonate and sulfate, grown over years from carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide in the air on a first film of Cu2O. That is atmospheric weathering, it needs a gas phase this bench does not carry, and no route here claims it
  Cu | Cu+2 ‖ Cu+2 | Cu: E_cell = 0.0220 V open-circuit, no current, no internal resistance modelled (E°_cell = 0.0000 V; the difference is the Nernst term over the computed ion activities; ideal salt bridge, no liquid-junction potential); anode v2, cathode v1; Cu + Cu+2 → Cu+2 + Cu
